<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ⚠ NOT IN USE. Kept for reference only — the live app is the ConnectedApp in
  ../connectedApps/Field_Triage.connectedApp-meta.xml.

  This ECA works, but only for users of the org that defines it. Authorizing
  from any other org fails with:

      OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_BLOCKED: Cross-org OAuth flows are not supported
      for this external client app

  which is fatal for a lead magnet — the entire point is that a stranger's org
  can connect without installing anything. Verified against two separate orgs.

  distributionState: Packaged would presumably lift that, but a packaged ECA has
  to be installed in the target org first, which is the same problem wearing a
  different hat.

  So: Connected Apps remain the only mechanism supporting anonymous cross-org
  OAuth, despite being deprecated. Revisit when Salesforce ships an ECA
  equivalent — the migration is otherwise done, and the notes below are correct.

  Split across four metadata types by design:
    ExternalClientApplication            the app itself (this file)
    ExtlClntAppGlobalOauthSettings       consumer key, callback, scopes
    ExtlClntAppOauthSettings             enables the OAuth plugin in this org
    ExtlClntAppOauthConfigurablePolicies who may authorize, IP relaxation
-->
<ExternalClientApplication xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <contactEmail>brendan@datajungle.io</contactEmail>
    <description>Free Salesforce field audit by Data Jungle. Reads field metadata and aggregate record counts only — never field values — and writes nothing.</description>
    <distributionState>Local</distributionState>
    <isProtected>false</isProtected>
    <label>Data Jungle Field Triage</label>
</ExternalClientApplication>
